<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesoamerican codices</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesoamerican codices are folding books painted on bark paper or deerskin by the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, recording history, religion, calendars and tribute. Many were destroyed by Spanish missionaries after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. The surviving codices, including some made under Spanish rule, are key sources for Aztec history and culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-codices.jpg" alt="Mesoamerican codices" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tlakwilo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Examples_of_Mesoamerican_pictorial_manuscripts_(facsimiles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
